            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Caracalla's Reign</strong><br>\nCaracalla's reign as sole emperor was notable for military campaigns and the construction of public works. He is best remembered for his Constitutio Antoniniana, also known as the Edict of Caracalla, which granted Roman citizenship to all free men within the empire. This edict was a radical move that significantly expanded the number of citizens who could be taxed and conscripted into the military.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Campaigns</strong><br>\nCaracalla was known for his military focus and spent much of his reign on campaign. He led his armies with a degree of personal bravery and zeal, often engaging in warfare in Germania and Parthia. His campaigns in the east were an attempt to secure the boundaries of the empire and replicate the successes of his father, though they met with mixed results.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Baths of Caracalla</strong><br>\nOne of Caracalla's most enduring legacies is the Baths of Caracalla in Rome, one of the largest and most elaborate bath complexes of antiquity. These baths showcased the grandeur of his reign and offered sophisticated recreational and bathing facilities to the public, featuring libraries, gardens, and rooms for exercise and study, illustrating Roman engineering and architectural excellence.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
