          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Legal Reforms</strong><br>\nAside from military endeavors, Caracalla enacted several legal reforms. He is known to have increased the pay of soldiers, reflecting his focus on the military and efforts to maintain loyalty among the troops. He also issued laws that were pragmatic and sometimes brutal, reflecting his authoritarian rule and the need to manage a vast and diverse empire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Assassination of Caracalla</strong><br>\nCaracalla's rule ended abruptly when he was assassinated on April 8, 217 AD, near Carrhae by a disaffected soldier. His assassination was part of a conspiracy that involved members of his own bodyguard and possibly the Praetorian Prefect Macrinus, who succeeded him. His death was violent and mirrored the brutality that had marked his reign.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Caracalla</strong><br>\nCaracalla is often remembered as a tyrant, with a reign defined by bloodshed, substantial military expenditure, and autocratic rule. However, his impact on Roman law and citizenship has had lasting effects on the historical development of the Roman state. Despite his controversial legacy, his decisions helped shape the structure of the late Roman Empire.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
